Absolutely. Here is a step-by-step Git command list with simple meanings, especially useful for DevOps interviews.
1. Configure Git

Set your name

git config --global user.name "Your Name"
Set your email:
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
Check configuration:
git config --list
Meaning: Tells Git who is creating the commits.

2. Create / Download a Repository
git init
git init
Meaning: Creates a new Git repository in your current directory.
git clone
git clone <repository-url>
Meaning: Downloads an existing remote repository to your local machine.

3. Check Repository Status
git status
Meaning: Shows modified, untracked, and staged files.
This is one of the most commonly used Git commands.

4. Working Directory → Staging Area
Suppose you modify:
main.tf
variables.tf
Check:
git status
git add
git add main.tf
Meaning: Moves main.tf from the working directory to the staging area.
To stage everything:
git add .
Flow:
Working Directory
       |
       | git add
       ↓
Staging Area

5. Commit Changes
git commit -m "Added EC2 Terraform configuration"
Meaning: Saves the staged changes into your local Git repository.
Flow:
Working Directory
       ↓ git add
Staging Area
       ↓ git commit
Local Repository

6. View Commit History
git log
Meaning: Shows commit history.
Short format:
git log --oneline
Example:
a1b2c3d Added EC2 instance
d4e5f6g Updated variables
h7i8j9k Initial commit

7. Create a Branch
git branch feature-login
Meaning: Creates a new branch.
Create and switch to it:
git checkout -b feature-login
Modern command:
git switch -c feature-login
8. Switch Branch
Old command:
git checkout main
Modern command:
git switch main
Meaning: Moves you from one branch to another.

9. List Branches
git branch
Meaning: Shows local branches.
To see remote branches:
git branch -r
All branches:
git branch -a

10. Delete a Branch
git branch -d feature-login
Meaning: Deletes a local branch after it has been merged.
Force delete:
git branch -D feature-login

11. Connect to Remote Repository
Check remote:
git remote -v
Add remote:
git remote add origin <repository-url>
Meaning: Connects your local repository to GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket.

12. Git Push
git push origin main
Meaning: Uploads your local commits to the remote repository.
For a new branch:
git push -u origin feature-login

13. Git Fetch
git fetch origin
Meaning: Downloads the latest information from the remote repository without automatically merging it into your current branch.
Easy way:
Fetch = Download and review.

14. Git Pull
git pull origin main
Meaning: Gets changes from the remote repository and integrates them into your current branch.
Easy way:
Pull = Fetch + integrate.

15. Git Merge
git checkout main
git merge feature-login
Meaning: Combines the changes from feature-login into main.
Easy way:
Merge = Combine branches.

16. Git Rebase
git checkout feature-login
git rebase main
Meaning: Replays your feature-branch commits on top of the latest main commits.
Used to maintain a cleaner, linear history.

17. Git Diff
git diff
Meaning: Shows changes that are in your working directory but haven't been staged.
Staged changes:
git diff --staged

18. Git Stash
git stash
Meaning: Temporarily saves your uncommitted changes.
Later:
git stash pop
Meaning: Restores the stashed changes.
View stashes:
git stash list
Easy way:
Stash = Temporarily keep unfinished work aside.

19. Git Reset
Unstage a file:
git reset filename
Meaning: Removes the file from the staging area but keeps your changes.

20. Git Restore
Discard changes in a file:
git restore filename
Meaning: Removes your uncommitted changes from that file and restores the last committed version.
⚠️ Be careful because your uncommitted changes can be lost.

21. Git RM
git rm filename
Meaning: Removes the file from your working directory and Git tracking.
To stop tracking but keep the file:
git rm --cached filename
This is commonly used with .gitignore.

22. .gitignore
Create:
.gitignore
Example:
.terraform/
*.tfstate
.env
*.log
Meaning: Tells Git which files/directories should not be tracked.

23. Git Cherry-pick
git cherry-pick <commit-id>
Meaning: Takes one specific commit from another branch and applies it to your current branch.
Example:
feature branch
      |
      D  ← bug fix
      |
      ↓
cherry-pick D
      |
      ↓
main
Easy way:
Cherry-pick = Bring only a specific commit.

24. Git Squash
Usually:
git rebase -i HEAD~3
Then change:
pick   Commit-1
pick   Commit-2
pick   Commit-3
to:
pick   Commit-1
squash Commit-2
squash Commit-3
Meaning: Combines multiple commits into one commit.
Easy way:
Squash = Combine commits.

25. Git Tag
git tag v1.0.0
Meaning: Creates a tag for a specific commit, commonly used for releases.
Push tag:
git push origin v1.0.0

26. Git Show
git show <commit-id>
Meaning: Shows the details and changes introduced by a particular commit.

27. Git Revert
git revert <commit-id>
Meaning: Creates a new commit that reverses the changes from an earlier commit.
This is generally safer than resetting commits that have already been pushed and shared.
Complete Git Workflow
This is the flow you should remember for interviews:
              Remote Repository
                     ↑
                  git push
                     |
              Local Repository
                     ↑
                git commit
                     |
                Staging Area
                     ↑
                  git add
                     |
              Working Directory

A practical workflow:
=====================

git clone <repo-url>

git checkout -b feature-login

# Make your changes

git status

git add .

git diff --staged

git commit -m "Added login feature"

git fetch origin

git rebase origin/main

git push -u origin feature-login

Then create a Pull Request/Merge Request, get the code reviewed, and merge the feature into the appropriate branch.

Most important commands for a DevOps interview
If you want to memorize only the important ones first:

git clone       → Download repository
git init        → Create repository
git status      → Check changes
git add         → Stage changes
git commit      → Save changes locally
git log         → View history
git branch      → Manage branches
git checkout    → Switch branch
git switch      → Switch branch
git fetch       → Download remote changes
git pull        → Download + integrate
git push        → Upload commits
git merge       → Combine branches
git rebase      → Replay commits / linear history
git stash       → Temporarily save changes
git cherry-pick → Apply specific commit
git revert      → Reverse a commit with a new commit
git reset       → Move/reset HEAD or unstage changes
git diff        → See changes
git tag         → Mark a release

Interview shortcut:
“The basic Git flow is: modify → git status → git add → git commit → git push. When working with a team, we use branches, fetch/pull, merge or rebase, and pull requests for code review.”